### Multi-phase or multi-PR plan

**You own the plan, not the code. The plan is a checklist an owner runs box by box and the operator audits from the evidence.** The plan is the deliverable. Do not implement.

1. Change is one or two files with an obvious approach? Skip the plan. Say so, stop.
2. Settle open questions by prototype before you write. Run [`playbooks/prototype.md`](prototype.md) for each. Keep the branch, SHA, screenshots for Appendix A. Ask the operator only about a product or preference call no run can settle. Give options (the **never-block-on-the-human** principle skill).
3. Explore with `saber-agent` subagents (the [context window guard](../../context-window-guard/SKILL.md) principle skill). Each returns file pointers, conventions, test commands, entry points. No inlined dumps.
4. Copy the skeleton below into the plan file, fill every placeholder. Unless the operator names a path, write under the agent store's `docs/`. Keep every heading and sub-block in the order shown. One section per PR. One PR = one change with its own evidence (the [sequence verifiable units](../../sequence-verifiable-units/SKILL.md) principle skill). Name the execution playbook in **How to read this**. Pick between [`playbooks/autopilot-full.md`](autopilot-full.md) and [`playbooks/autopilot-stack.md`](autopilot-stack.md) per the rule at the end of [`playbooks/autopilot-stack.md`](autopilot-stack.md). A standing program takes [`playbooks/orchestrate.md`](orchestrate.md).
5. Write under [`/technical-writing`](../../technical-writing/SKILL.md) in full, then [`/unslop`](../../unslop/SKILL.md). Body = one Diátaxis mode, how-to. Appendices hold explanation and reference. Each heading states the task or finding. No long dashes. No mid-sentence colons.
6. Re-read the plan against this playbook's structure, fix every drift (the [encode lessons in structure](../../encode-lessons-in-structure/SKILL.md) principle skill).
7. Hand back. Post the plan path and the script's output, stop. Execution starts on the operator's explicit go, under the execution playbook the plan names.

**Verification.** Tests alone are not sufficient verification. A PR is verified only when its unit, live, and perf boxes are all checked (the [prove it works](../../prove-it-works/SKILL.md) principle skill). That sentence is the verification rule. Every verification block opens with it. Live block mandatory. Ten lanes at the PR head drive the real surface through its control skill, per the [swarm](../../swarm/SKILL.md) skill. Each lane: one box with a concrete scenario, the screenshot it saves, its pass predicate. One lane is the **Regression lane against trunk.** Same load-bearing scenario on trunk and head. Trunk lacks the feature? Record that, gate the behavior the diff adds plus the end state the user waits for, don't invent a trunk result. Perf gate dual-sided: trunk and head must both produce the named metric. Trunk lacks the feature? Also isolate the diff-added work, set an absolute budget for it plus the end-to-end state the user waits for. Don't claim a ratio between unlike scenarios. The perf block names the metric, the interleaved probe, the trunk baseline measured first, the rule with the number that fails. A PR that changes an interaction is review-gated: operator reviews in chat with screenshots and a video before merge. A PR that changes no interaction writes `**Review gate.** None. <PR id> is not review-gated.` and no boxes under it.

**Control skill.** Pick by surface. Browser, Electron, web UIs, CLIs, TUIs all route through the [drive surface](../../drive-surface/SKILL.md) skill. A PR touching two surfaces gets lanes on both. A surface with no control skill is a risk in Appendix C; its live block still names how each lane drives it.

